# The challenges and opportunities of hybrid work in large BSS organizations

More than two years of experience with hybrid work, already shows that it presents significant challenges, and the top ones to address, are:

a. Inconsistent employee experience: Hybrid work can create disparities in employee experience and access to resources, leading to potential feelings of inequity among the workforce.
b. Difficulty in fostering collaboration and team cohesion: Remote work can hinder team collaboration and cohesion, as employees have fewer opportunities for face-to-face interactions and spontaneous conversations.
c. Increased management complexity: Managing a hybrid workforce requires new skill sets and strategies, which can be challenging for managers to develop and implement effectively.

By carefully considering the pros and cons of hybrid work, BSS organizations can develop a well-balanced work model that supports employee well-being and productivity. Definitely one of the top challenges for managers is the creation of a culture of trust and collaboration among remote and in-office employees.

So why to adopt hybrid work? Well, as we all know already, being employed with hybrid models definitely allows for:

a. Flexibility: Hybrid work offers employees greater flexibility, allowing them to balance their personal and professional lives more effectively, and improving overall job satisfaction.
b. Access to a wider talent pool: By adopting hybrid work, organizations can tap into a broader talent pool, not limited by geographical constraints, and attract top talent from diverse backgrounds.
c. Cost savings: Over time, organizations can achieve cost savings through reduced office space requirements and lower overhead expenses related to in-office employees.

The Future of work in the BSS sector is going to be a combination of remote and in-office arrangements. As we can already observe, this is unavoidable, and the key challenges now are to adapt to it and build a more agile, diverse, and resilient workforce.

